## Linux Directories Explained

# The Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS)

Linux does not use drive letters like Windows (`C:\`, `D:\`). Everything in Linux starts from a single root directory, represented by a forward slash `/`. All files, directories, virtual hardware interfaces, and mounted drives branch out from this single root.


## The Root Directory (`/`)

The absolute top level of the entire file system hierarchy. Every file and directory stems from here.

```
[ / Root Directory ] ➔ [ Subdirectories ] ➔ [ Files & System Data ]

```

## Core System & Binary Directories

These directories hold the essential programs and binaries required for the operating system to boot and run.

```
[ / ] ➔ [ /bin & /sbin ] ➔ [ Core System Executables ]

```

### Essential Directories

* `/bin` and `/sbin` store primary command-line binaries and administrative commands like `ls`, `cp`, `ip`, and `systemctl` (in modern RHEL, these are symlinked to `/usr/bin` and `/usr/sbin`).
* `/boot` contains the Linux kernel, initial RAM disk images (initramfs), and GRUB bootloader files needed to boot the OS.
* `/lib` and `/lib64` hold shared code libraries required by system binaries to execute properly.


## User & Variable Data Directories

System users, applications, and dynamic processes read and write data across these isolated structures.

```
[ / ] ➔ [ /home & /var ] ➔ [ User Files & Active Log Data ]

```

### Essential Directories

* `/home` contains personal home directories for standard users (e.g., `/home/john`), housing individual configurations and personal files.
* `/root` is the dedicated home directory for the administrative superuser (`root`), kept separate from standard user directories on `/home`.
* `/var` stores variable data that changes constantly during system operation, such as log files (`/var/log`), mail queues, and database files.
* `/tmp` provides temporary file storage accessible to all applications; files here are automatically cleared on reboot or periodic cleanup.


## Configuration & System Software Directories

Administrative settings and secondary software packages live within these dedicated areas.

```
[ / ] ➔ [ /etc & /usr ] ➔ [ System Configurations & Application Files ]

```

### Essential Directories

* `/etc` houses all system-wide configuration files and plain-text settings (e.g., `/etc/fstab`, `/etc/passwd`, `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`).
* `/usr` holds user binaries, documentation, and read-only system software installed by the package manager (`/usr/bin`, `/usr/share/doc`).
* `/opt` reserved for optional or third-party add-on software packages that do not follow standard package installation paths.


## Virtual & Hardware Directories

Linux treats hardware, devices, and kernel processes as files through virtual filesystems created in RAM at runtime.

```
[ Kernel Memory ] ➔ [ /proc & /sys ] ➔ [ System & Hardware State ]

```

### Essential Directories

* `/dev` contains special device files representing connected hardware components (e.g., `/dev/sda` for hard drives, `/dev/null` for data disposal).
* `/proc` a virtual filesystem containing active process information and kernel parameters rendered directly from memory.
* `/sys` a virtual filesystem exposing hardware parameters, driver information, and device attributes to the operating system.
* `/mnt` and `/media` temporary mount points used by administrators to attach external file systems, network drives, or removable media.